    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, CURRENT] Reviewed 2026-11-01 at dev 100b6fbb6e. entrypoints.py still clamps the connectivity boost with min(1.0, ...) and sorts by (rank_score, _language_weight, _effective_out_degree); _diversity_cap is unchanged. Fixture (80 Flask routes + an Express scanner/server.js with POST /scan, GET /health): 50 entrypoints are kept, 48 http_route all at rank 1.0 and all Python; both Express routes are dropped (alone they rank 1.0); the sketch HTTP Routes section lists 48 app.py routes and no Express route. Correction: the tie-break is not pure insertion order; the second key, language weight, is what pushes the minority-language service out. (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nFound while fixing INV-liraj. On thc after the fix, 291 of 372 http_route entrypoints tie at rank_score 1.0 (base 0.90/0.95 + connectivity boost, capped at 1.0), so their order is insertion order. Two consequences: (1) the entrypoint cap (495; _diversity_cap gives http_route 198 + overflow) keeps 240 routes and drops the Express service's POST /scan (rank 1.0) and GET /health (0.989) from scanner/server.js, which were the only routes listed before the fix -- a second service's routes are crowded out by an arbitrary tie order; (2) the sketch's HTTP Routes section shows the first 19 routes in router order (17 of 19 are LIVE routes).\n\nProposal to evaluate: a tie-break that spreads the cap/sketch selection across files/frameworks (round-robin by route file) and/or avoids boost saturation for routes. Measure on thc and one multi-service repo."  # 3efb
- op: discuss  # 6473
  at: '2026-11-14T05:05:56Z'  # 6473
  by: agent  # 6473
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6473
  clock: 4  # 6473
  nonce: '6473'  # 6473
  message: "Second outside-reader review, 2026-11-13 (self-run cache f36c63310c791188/results/c872eb7a7e793eab/ac34c56c06ec1788, re-checked on dev fed15705d7, 20 other repositories measured; ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/outside_reader_review2_11142026). NEW INSTANCE on the self-run: the ten highest CLI entries all have rank_score exactly 1.0 (a main_function at base 0.8 and a cli_command at base 0.99 both clamp), so their order is the tie-break: language weight, then effective out-degree. The console scripts declared in [project.scripts] (hypergumbo-tracker, htrac, hypergumbo, hypergumbo-tracker-textconv) are symbols of language toml, whose weight is near zero, so they come 7th to 10th, under every saturated Python main including the main of a recorded test-input project (6th). The tracker's main is first and the core main second on effective out-degree (380 against 89). A reader saw this as 'the hypergumbo console script is ninth, below the tracker's main'. So the highest-confidence statement a repository makes about its own commands ranks last among the ties. Read from the entrypoints of the full survey."  # 6473
- op: discuss  # 3c7b
  at: '2026-11-14T09:47:52Z'  # 3c7b
  by: agent  # 3c7b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3c7b
  clock: 5  # 3c7b
  nonce: 3c7b  # 3c7b
  message: "WHY PR #2183 DOES NOT TOUCH THIS. The ties come from the clamp at 1.0 and from the boost's own cap (0.25, reached at 11 effective edges). Removing either is not a local fix: docs/schema.json bounds Entrypoint.rank_score at 1.0, and ADR-0039 ruling 3 moves the ranking adjustments to rank_score 'unchanged in formula and magnitude'. A formula that keeps the base order of detection kinds under the bound (rank = base + (1 - base) x boost share) still ties at 1.0 once the boost is saturated. So this needs a ruling on the rank formula, then a measurement of the entry order on a corpus."  # 3c7b
- op: discuss  # 4a27
  at: '2026-11-15T21:56:49Z'  # 4a27
  by: agent  # 4a27
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4a27
  clock: 6  # 4a27
  nonce: 4a27  # 4a27
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-11-15 (on my recommendation, plan: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/stable_id_items_review_11152026/PLAN.md, PR 3). Leave rank_score, its 1.0 ceiling and the schema bound as they are. Change two things. (1) The tie-break: detection confidence first, then the keys used now (language weight, effective out-degree), so a manifest-declared script (0.99) comes before a framework route (0.95 or 0.90) and before a main found by convention (0.80). (2) The cap: when it cuts through a group of entries with equal score, deal the slots round-robin across source files, so every file with a tied entry gets one before any file gets a second. WHY NOT THE SCORE: removing the ceiling would not bring back a minority-language service, because the connectivity boost is already halved for a minority language, so its routes would score below every route of the larger language. ADR-0039 ruling 3 froze the score formula; the order among equal scores and the cap are outside it, and the ADR gets a sentence saying so. NOT BUILT, NOT MEASURED: entry order before and after on the 20 repositories of the second outside-reader review plus thc, and the bakeoff cases the code names (forgejo, DMD, gemini-cli) where a clone exists. WI-fosuk lands first in the same PR because it changes which entries this orders."  # 4a27
